ed party have  some changes in the in the cabinet  rather fewer people saying yes of course  boris and a few more saying are you nuts  prime minister would be helpful  um if he can do that he might just start  to win back the confidence but there's a  big question mark over there so you'd  like to see a reshuffle effectively with  people who are prepared to stand up to  him a bit more i i think we need to have  a cabinet as we've had before if you go  back to mrs thatcher's time she didn't  uh surround herself with yes men and  women or aren't any women it was people  that she fundamentally disagreed with  but would take them on people who would  say to her are you sure that's what you  want to do actually i think you're wrong  and she would say well i think i'm right  and this is why we need more of that  sort of debate in in cabinet we need far  more backbenchers there's huge talent on  the back benches who are not playing any  parting government at the at the moment  who have got a lot to offer so i think  boris has got to absolutely extend the  hand of friendship and be much more  inclusive to many more people in the  party where there is a lot of talent  that's not being used is there any names  that you would give us for examples of  those kind of no but no no because then  you'll start getting into um arguments  out why him and not her or whatever um i  think backbenchers feel a bit neglected  and i think the clear-up that happened  in number 10 had to happen but it hasn't  gone um far enough we need to be  talked to a lot more we need to be  consulted about policy um a lot more and  we don't need this problem where we're  marched up to the top of the hill to  defend things which are  a bit tricky indefensible some would say  some would say indefensible only for 24  hours later number 10 to do a complete  u-turn and we marched all the way down  again these are the sorts of things that  undermined confidence in even david  cameron and theresa may um previously so  we've got a huge challenge facing us the  country's got a huge challenge with the  cost of living that crisis with the  continuing issues around ukraine and  lots of other other things we need  absolutely to be focused on that and  delivering for the people not having  debates amongst ourselves about whether  our leader is up to being leader or not  are there policy changes that you would  like to see as well well i don't think  this is large about policies if you look  at previous prime ministers that were  got rid of  poll tax mrs thatcher started the the  slide there  this hasn't been about policy  i'm afraid it's been about  the way the prime minister has  personally handled what started as a  party gate and for me it was never about  how many glasses of warm chardonnay were  coffeed in downing street or sausage  rolls eaten it was about the way it was  handled um and that then became the  issue rather than the so-called parties  them that themselves and by the way it  was handled do you mean um lack of  contrition or or not really accepting  but i think a lot of contrition and i  think as one of your panelists actually  said earlier if he at the beginning had  actually owned it and said  actually i think we've we might have um  committed some offenses here actually i  didn't realize but in retrospect um i  think we shouldn't have done it this way  and so we need to investigate if we've  actually committed any  crimes um at all and absolutely owned it  from the start i think he put his hands  up rather than trying to say no no  nothing went wrong there were no parties  oh there were parties but they're all  within the rules and oh but i wasn't at  any of these parties but then actually i  might have been a party and that just  the credibility just just drifted that  was the problem so getting back that  that confidence of people  that his integrity is in uh intact uh  and that he's capable of carrying on  with this job is going to be a big big  challenge but people want to see a prime  minister and the government getting on  with the job of the challenges facing  everybody in their everyday lives which  aren't considerable i just wonder what  you make of the prime minister's  reaction to the confidence vote he's  described it as a very good result and  decisive victory do you think he gets it  well he clearly wasn't i mean the margin  was much tighter than most people had  expected and certainly he had had wanted  and again i think he needs to own that i  think he needs to say look okay we have  got a problem which is why i think he  needs to bring people inside the tent  who have opposed him in some cases uh  candidates who stood against him who  frankly should have been in the cabinet  right from the  from the start to try and get unity  together again and get those people  firing all cylinders at the jobs that  all those cabinet ministers need to be  doing in a government that's delivering  for the people  i'm quite intrigued as to  whether or not some of the rebels are  likely to have another go  according to the current rules of the  1922 committee the prime minister now  can't be challenged for a year but of  course those rules can be changed  is that an active discussion  no and i think what's different about  this there's a really interesting piece  in the times by william hague yesterday  about  when there are plots to get rid of um of  leaders this wasn't an organized one  where there was a clear alternative  contender this was quite a few people  who were a bit discontented you know  like like me and others thinking  actually we've got to do better than  this there wasn't so some organized  campaign i think a few people like to  claim that they were running organized  campaign it didn't actually um happen  like that the rules are the rules we've  had a result under the rules that result  stands it doesn't look good if within  days of that result you tried to start  changing the rules so i'm not really  interested in that  if the prime minister absolutely  realizes that his position  continues to be untenable as some people  have said and i said back in in january  then it's for him to make the decision  to resign until there could possibly be  a challenge a year out we're getting  closer to an election  people don't elect parties that are  divided we need a strong clear leader  with strong leadership with a good solid  cabinet and ministers firing on all  cylinders that's his challenge in the  next few weeks if he's to stand any  chance of going to next election as the  leader of the conservative party and  prime minister what do you think his  chances are of going into that election  i i honestly don't know it's really  difficult to uh to tell because all  sorts of things have happened in the  last few months that have come out of  come out of nowhere  um  and i think if there are more party gate  type  mishaps to put it mildly  um then his position becomes more  untenable you know the trouble is this  time many of us have people writing just  saying that  the behavior that promises has been  absolutely disgraceful but they've said  that about the last conservative prime  minister or anything the conservative  government does there have been quite a  few  actual conservatives who said look i've  voted conservative all my life and i  know they're conservative as my  constituency and i can't do so  absolutely so he's got to be able to win  those people back  to stand a fighting chance of being a  successful leader to win the next  election for us and the jury is out on  on that but he's the leader we've  reelected  him as the leader we need to get on with  the job of him as prime minister by  government that's doing stuff that's  outside of westminster not looking on  ourselves

